# Clara's Part 2 fixes (5 Oct 2026), simplified for reading level.
NEW = {
"How much can be paid in each year?": ["For 2026/27 the standard annual allowance is £60,000 across all your pensions. For a money purchase pension, like most SSASs, it counts what you, your company and anyone else pay in during the year. Above it, a tax charge may apply. Tax relief on your own payments is normally limited to your UK earnings, or £3,600 if that is more. Your company's payments are not capped by your salary. But the annual allowance and the corporation tax rules still apply.", "The allowance is lower if your threshold income is over £200,000 and your adjusted income is over £260,000. It never goes below £10,000. It is also £10,000 for money purchase savings once you have taken money flexibly from a pension."],
"Does my company get corporation tax relief?": ["Usually, yes. Your company can deduct its pension payments from its profits. It gets the relief for the accounting period in which it actually pays them. HMRC accepts this unless there is a non-business reason for the payment. One example is a director's total pay being too high for the work they do.", "Very large increases can be spread. This happens if this period's payments are more than 2.1 times the last period's. HMRC then works out the excess over 1.1 times the last period's payments. If that excess is £500,000 or more, the relief on it is spread over 2, 3 or 4 years. Giving the scheme an asset instead of money is not normally a contribution that gets relief. HMRC accepts a cash contribution settled by selling an asset to the scheme at market value only if the debt, the sale and the set-off are each agreed and recorded at the time."],
"Can I move my old pensions into a SSAS?": ["The law allows money to move from one registered pension scheme to another. A SSAS can accept it if its rules allow. Before your old scheme lets the money go, it must run the scam checks in the 2021 transfer rules. These include proof that you have a real link to the SSAS's employer. Some transfer advice is regulated by the FCA, especially advice about final salary pensions. Anyone giving regulated investment advice must have the right FCA permission. Moving can mean losing valuable benefits or guarantees in your old scheme."],
"What can a SSAS invest in?": ["HMRC's tax rules do not limit the kinds of asset a SSAS can buy. But some bring heavy tax charges. These are homes, and physical things you can move, such as art, antiques or classic cars. Pension law also gives the trustees duties. Before most investments, even in a small SSAS, they must get and consider proper advice, given or confirmed in writing. They must also think about spreading the risk where appropriate."],
"Can a SSAS own residential property?": ["It can, but homes are taxable property and the tax charges are heavy. The member can face a 40% charge, with a possible 15% surcharge. The scheme faces a scheme sanction charge. There are also yearly charges while the scheme holds the home, and a charge on any gain.", "Some buildings do not count as homes, such as qualifying care homes, student halls, hospitals and a whole hotel. Some homes linked to a job or to business premises can also be left out, but strict conditions apply. For example, the person living there must not be a member or connected with one. For a flat for an employee, the employee must not be connected with the employer either."],
"Can my SSAS borrow?": ["Yes. A SSAS can borrow up to 50% of its net value just before the new borrowing. Any borrowing it already has counts towards that limit. HMRC lets it borrow for any purpose the trustees are satisfied will benefit the scheme. If new borrowing takes the scheme over the limit, the excess normally faces a 40% scheme sanction charge. If existing borrowing was already over the limit, the whole of the new borrowing can be charged. Pension law's own limit on borrowing does not apply to schemes with fewer than 100 members."],
"Can my SSAS lend money to my company?": ["Yes, if your company is a sponsoring employer and the loan meets HMRC's strict rules (see <a href=\"#what-are-the-loanback-rules\">the loanback rules</a>). The SSAS cannot lend to you, to any other member, or to anyone connected with a member. Pension law also bans loans to the employer unless the SSAS meets the small scheme conditions. That means fewer than 12 members, all of them trustees (or directors of a company that is the only trustee). The trustees must also decide everything together, or have the required independent trustee."],
"What are the loanback rules?": ["A loan to a sponsoring employer can be no more than 50% of the SSAS's net value when it is made. It must be secured by a first charge for its whole term. The asset charged must be worth at least the loan plus interest. Interest must be at least 1% above the average base rate of six named banks. That average is first rounded up to the next 0.25%. The loan must be repaid within five years. By the end of each loan year, at least a minimum total of capital and interest must have been repaid. Equal yearly amounts meet this, and paying more early is fine too. HMRC's guidance does not say what the company may spend the money on. But every loan must be a genuine investment: prudent, secure and commercial."],
"Can my SSAS lend to somebody else's company?": ["Yes. Any registered pension scheme may lend to someone who is not connected with it, on genuine commercial terms. The loan should be a genuine investment: prudent, secure and commercial. A loan to a member, or to someone connected with a member, is normally an unauthorised payment. Connected is a wide idea. It includes close relatives, husbands, wives and civil partners and their relatives, and companies any of them control. A loan to someone connected only with a sponsoring employer counts as a loan to that employer, so it must meet the loanback rules."],
"Can a SSAS buy shares in an unquoted company?": ["Yes. HMRC says pension schemes can buy shares whether or not they are quoted on a stock exchange. The seller might be a member, the employer or someone connected with them. If so, the price must be what an unconnected buyer would pay. If the company owns homes, the SSAS can be treated as owning part of them. That does not apply if all four of these are true. The company's main activity is a trade, profession or vocation. The SSAS and people linked to it do not control it. No member or connected person is a controlling director. And the shares are not held so that a member or connected person can use the property."],
"Can a SSAS pay for life assurance?": ["Yes. HMRC says pension schemes can use insurance policies, such as term life cover, to fund the death benefits they promise. The insurer pays the policy money to the scheme. The trustees then pay whatever death benefit the scheme rules promise, which may be a lump sum or a pension. A member's own contributions used to pay premiums on a personal (non-group) life policy get no tax relief. From 6 April 2027, death in service benefits paid by a registered pension scheme stay outside inheritance tax, if they meet the conditions."],
}
ADD = {
"How much can be paid in each year?": ["SRC: Law | Finance Act 2004, ss.188 and 189 | https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2004/12/section/188"],
"Can my SSAS borrow?": ["SRC: Law | Finance Act 2004, s.241 | https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2004/12/section/241"],
"Can my SSAS lend to somebody else's company?": ["SRC: Law | Finance Act 2004, s.161 | https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2004/12/section/161",
                                                "SRC: HMRC | PTM123200: loans to sponsoring employers | https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/pensions-tax-manual/ptm123200"],
"Can my SSAS buy shares in my company?": ["SRC: Law | Finance Act 2004, s.180(5) | https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2004/12/section/180"],
"Can a SSAS own residential property?": [],
}
lines=open('faq.txt',encoding='utf-8').read().splitlines()
out=[];i=0;done=set()
while i<len(lines):
    l=lines[i]; out.append(l); i+=1
    if l.startswith('## '):
        q=l[3:].strip(); block=[]
        while i<len(lines) and not lines[i].startswith('## ') and not lines[i].startswith('# '):
            if lines[i].strip(): block.append(lines[i])
            i+=1
        srcs=[b for b in block if b.startswith('SRC:')]
        paras=[b for b in block if not b.startswith('SRC:')]
        if q in NEW: paras=NEW[q]; done.add(q)
        for extra in ADD.get(q,[]):
            if extra not in srcs: srcs.append(extra)
        out.extend(paras+srcs); out.append('')
assert done==set(NEW), set(NEW)-done
open('faq.txt','w',encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(out).rstrip()+'\n')
print('patched',len(done))
